ntation due to losses during treatment.\n\nVITAMINA B12 is typically monitored in patients with cystic fibrosis and those receiving parenteral nutrition, as absorption issues can arise in these populations. It is included in various multivitamin formulations, such as Infuvite Pediátrico, to support overall health. The vitamin is found naturally in foods such as liver, eggs, milk, and fish, and specific intake recommendations exist to ensure adequate levels.\n\nDespite its importance, upper limits for VITAMINA B12 intake have not been established due to a lack of data on adverse effects. The recommended daily intake is approximately 5 micrograms. VITAMINA B12 works in conjunction with folate to help prevent elevated homocysteine levels, which are associated with various health risks. \n\nIn summary, VITAMINA B12 is an essential nutrient that requires careful monitoring and supplementation in certain populations to maintain optimal health and prevent deficiencies.\",33\r\n4412,METFORMINA,\"Metformina, or metformin, is a medication primarily used for the treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus. It works by improving insulin sensitivity, which helps manage blood sugar levels in patients with this condition. However, in some cases, such as when a patient has low glomerular filtration rates, metformin may be suspended due to potential risks. Additionally, it is recommended that metformin be withdrawn two days prior to any studies if gastrointestinal tumors are suspected.\n\nWhile metformin is effective in managing diabetes, it can be associated with side effects, including diarrhea and a reduction in vitamin B12 concentrations. In clinical studies, such as the ADOPT study, metformin has been compared to other medications like rosiglitazone, particularly in terms of glycemic durability. Overall, metformin remains a widely used and important medication in the management of type 2 diabetes, although careful consideration of patient-specific factors is necessary for its use.\",